# Not dying -- returning. The cell has no exit because the story
# does not need one: "every morning the same: you always return
# eventually," and the night in the cell ends with the game itself
# carrying you back to the inn bedroom. The walkthroughs' bedroom
# scene -- the woman, the coin, the unlocked door -- was transcribed
# twenty lines too early, against the FIRST morning, and refused
# there; this is the morning it belongs to. She has the key: pay
# her the gold coin and she unlocks the door on her way out.

wait
stand
open blind
kiss her
talk to woman
take money
give coin to her
talk to her
dress
open door
out
read note
destroy note

# The note names the mission -- "Our traitor is Giuseppe
# Florantine, aide to the Denizen. You may dispatch him any way you
# choose" -- and from here the game rail-guards the approach: the
# alley's other exits refuse ("Not turning back, now"), south opens
# into Piazza St. Marco, and the Palace door leads to the Grand
# Staircase, Florantine's door at its top. The walkthrough does not
# diverge here; it just never wrote the walk to work.

out
d
s
enter palace

# From here to the end, the game is DESIGNED to feel broken, and
# that design fooled everyone including this recording's makers.
# On the staircase the narrative dissociates: something else takes
# the body, and every typed command is echoed back a beat late --
# "... examine the room key ... the room key ..." -- while the
# story fast-forwards through a replay of everything that happened
# before. The refusals in this stretch are the scene's own voice;
# ride it. It ends in the Empty Room where the game began, with
# Giuseppe Florantine bound beside you, begging.

